PQClean vendoring record for src/pqcrypto/ml-dsa-65 (ML-DSA-65, FIPS 204) and src/pqcrypto/common
==================================================================================================

Upstream    https://github.com/PQClean/PQClean
Path        crypto_sign/ml-dsa-65/clean/  -- every .c and .h file and LICENSE (the Makefiles and
            META.yml were not copied; the file list is in src/CMakeLists.txt)
Also        common/fips202.c, common/fips202.h, common/randombytes.h, common/compat.h
            -> src/pqcrypto/common/  (common/sha2.c and sha2.h arrived later with SLH-DSA, see
            ../slh-dsa-sha2-128s/PQCLEAN; common/randombytes.c was vendored too and has since
            been removed, see below)
Commit      NOT RECORDED when the directory was vendored (this repository's commit 37c4741,
            "NEX -- post-quantum Bitcoin fork"). The files carry no version marker of their own.
            The only evidence in the tree: when SLH-DSA was vendored from PQClean master commit
            0586a824fc0d49df0b6b6e9179d8d15d06d0974f (fetched 2026-09-05), the common/ files
            already present from this directory's vendoring were found byte-identical to that
            commit's, so ML-DSA-65 was most likely taken from the same or a nearby master commit.
            Anyone re-vendoring must diff the whole directory against upstream rather than rely
            on this guess.
License     MIT (LICENSE in this directory; the upstream reference implementation is public domain
            / CC0 and PQClean's packaging is MIT)
KAT hash    None is claimed. No PQClean nistkat hash was ever computed for these files, and the
            sources are modified (below), so an upstream nistkat hash for
            crypto_sign/ml-dsa-65/clean does not apply to this directory. The correctness
            evidence is NIST's own FIPS 204 ACVP vectors pinned in
            src/test/data/ml_dsa_65_fips204.json and checked by src/test/ml_dsa_kat_tests.cpp:
            all 10 keyGen vectors reproduce NIST's public and secret keys byte for byte from the
            seed, all 15 sigVer vectors accept and reject exactly as NIST says, and an OpenSSL
            3.6.4 signature over a 32-byte message with an empty context (the chain's signing
            mode) verifies through CPQPubKey::Verify.

THE SOURCES ARE MODIFIED. Local changes, all inline (there is no patch file):
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. sign.c, sign.h, api.h: two functions added (this repository's commit 9d8b1ad):
     PQCLEAN_MLDSA65_CLEAN_crypto_sign_keypair_from_seed(pk, sk, seed)
       crypto_sign_keypair with the 32-byte FIPS 204 KeyGen_internal seed xi supplied by the
       caller instead of drawn from randombytes (deterministic HD key derivation);
     PQCLEAN_MLDSA65_CLEAN_crypto_sign_sk_to_pk(pk, sk)
       recomputes (rho, t1) from an unpacked secret key.
   Neither touches the existing functions; keygen and verification agree with FIPS 204 (above).

2. sign.c: the return value of randombytes() is checked in crypto_sign_keypair and
   crypto_sign_signature_ctx and both fail closed with -1 (upstream ignores it and would
   continue with an uninitialised buffer). Commit 9049774 ("H-03").

3. sign.c: secret intermediates are wiped with PQCLEAN_cleanse (below) on every exit path:
   seedbuf (xi / rhoprime / K / rnd), s1, s1hat, s2, t0 in keypair, keypair_from_seed and
   sk_to_pk; seedbuf, s1, s2, t0, y, z, w0 in crypto_sign_signature_ctx. The earlier
   memset()-based wipes on the failure paths were dead-store-eliminated by the optimizer and
   never executed in the built object. Signatures and verification are unaffected (the wipes
   run after the outputs are written).

4. common/cleanse.c, common/cleanse.h: NEW, not in PQClean. PQCLEAN_cleanse(ptr, len) is a
   memset followed by an asm memory barrier (SecureZeroMemory on Windows), the same construct as
   the node's memory_cleanse (src/support/cleanse.cpp), so the stores cannot be removed as dead.

5. common/fips202.h, common/fips202.c: the Keccak state is an inline array in every context
   struct (uint64_t ctx[26] / ctx[25]) instead of a malloc'd pointer. Upstream mallocs the state
   in every *_inc_init / *_absorb / *_ctx_clone and calls exit(111) when malloc returns NULL,
   which would terminate the node from a script-verification thread under memory exhaustion.
   Now no function in this file can fail or allocate, and *_ctx_release wipes the state with
   PQCLEAN_cleanse instead of freeing it. The API (function names, argument types, the
   PQC_SHAKE*CTX_BYTES macros) and every hash output are unchanged; only sizeof(the context
   structs) grew. The same change was applied to common/sha2.h and common/sha2.c (recorded in
   ../slh-dsa-sha2-128s/PQCLEAN, which vendored those two files).

6. common/randombytes.c: REMOVED from the tree. common/randombytes.h is unchanged and still
   declares PQCLEAN_randombytes; the definition is src/pqrandom.cpp (node code, built into
   bitcoin_util), which serves every request from the node's GetStrongRandBytes in 32-byte
   chunks and returns 0. PQClean's own file called the OS RNG directly (getrandom / getentropy /
   BCryptGenRandom), bypassing the node's RNG.

7. common/compat.h: `#if __clang__major__ < 9` corrected to `__clang_major__`. Upstream tests an
   undefined macro (always 0, so the pre-clang-9 vector-type polyfill was always emitted under
   clang, and -Wundef warns). No effect on any output.

8. Build (src/CMakeLists.txt, target pqcrypto): warnings are no longer suppressed with -w; the
   target builds with PQClean's own warning set (minus -Wvla, see ../slh-dsa-sha2-128s/PQCLEAN)
   and the node's hardening flags (_FORTIFY_SOURCE=3, -fstack-protector-all, -fcf-protection,
   -fstack-clash-protection, branch protection where the C compiler supports them) and with the
   sanitizer flags of a sanitizer build. The ML-DSA-65 sources compile without a warning under
   that set on Apple clang 21 (arm64).

Re-vendoring: copy crypto_sign/ml-dsa-65/clean/*.{c,h} and the common/ files listed above from
the chosen upstream commit, record that commit here, re-apply items 1-3 and 5-7 by hand
(diff this directory against the previous vendored copy to see them), do NOT re-add
common/randombytes.c to the build, rebuild, and run
`test_bitcoin -t ml_dsa_kat_tests -t pqkey_tests -t xcoin_v3_tests -t xcoin_descriptor_tests`.
